{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2630 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "53ce09d03be311e49cd41ec6e50f35552b9e5f07b0c3e53b83590e17406969af" :edb/db-before-t 2629 :edb/db-after-t 2630 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535942 50 #inst "2026-10-09T11:08:05.820Z" 13194139535942 true] [17592186151863 1100 "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2}, P_{2} = {1, 2, 3}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$." 13194139535942 false] [17592186151863 1100 "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186151863 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/94f8ae5652f9bf448a84bd30a23dbe09.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum,\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=1\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/c32290b459f231155ebed12f7dbb2413.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/e53b15ce6facb291ce49ed2b43c131db.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{11} &= f(1, 2) = 5 \\\\\nM_{21} &= f(2, 2) = 6 \\\\\nM_{12} &= f(1, 3) = 7 \\\\\nM_{22} &= f(2, 3) = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 = 26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 false] [17592186151863 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum,\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/26dc2a5af4c82657d9ec6fa331d4f5bc7575367bd4e3a00a5929b4ecaffe4477.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6d34737c12c35f6faec5caf37be500ba16c3f7cb18bf5ae660dc6d54182b66da.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=5 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =5+6+7+8=26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186151864 1100 "Let $R = [1,5] \\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {1, 3, 5}, P_{2} = {2, 4, 6}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$." 13194139535942 false] [17592186151864 1100 "Let $R = [1,5] \\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,3,5},\\,{P}_{2}={2,4,6},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186151864 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/9b2abc9b7e3a6bfcb6d7041a24fce9aa.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=4\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ regions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 4 \\cdot M_{11} + 4 \\cdot M_{21} + 4 \\cdot M_{12} + 4 \\cdot M_{22} \\\\\n&= 4(M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/143b73cdc2be16101dddbb200c3c39b6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/09e5f4950e59ffc259e3ed3bef452a2f.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{11} &= f(1, 2) = 60 \\\\\nM_{21} &= f(3, 2) = 52 \\\\\nM_{12} &= f(1, 4) = 48 \\\\\nM_{22} &= f(3, 4) = 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 4(60 + 52 + 48 + 40) = 800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 false] [17592186151864 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/5133b73c3707b87725baeee9a34a64898d33056dab9d7d32ac8cc24dbe772321.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ regions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/5b711e2c33e8966dfc9f4274a8f556f712d51a450bbcd0cadb68771fd050d304.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/ad83cf465252b1f880757405ca3ff9ab5e1b9b1e50d712bd3b81305b7973af27.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=60 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,2)=52 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,4)=48 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,4)=40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(60+52+48+40)=800.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186151865 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/33269c79413f4435d074d90cd727f2a53e7b9831300bfcaf0b5a7122df764080.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Find an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" 13194139535942 true] [17592186151865 1100 "Consider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R$. Find an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/7bd8e396e842ee10967b4656ad76cdd5.png)\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" 13194139535942 false] [17592186151865 1145 "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}$.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU = M_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21})\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = 8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU &= 8 \\cdot M_{11} + 4 \\cdot M_{21} \\\\\n&= 8M_{11} + 4M_{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 false] [17592186151865 1145 "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU = M_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = 8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =8\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21} \\\\  & =8{M}_{11}+4{M}_{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186152073 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375534 13194139535942 true] [17592186152073 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375562 13194139535942 true] [17592186152073 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375569 13194139535942 true] [17592186152073 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375576 13194139535942 true] [17592186152073 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375583 13194139535942 true] [17592186152074 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375541 13194139535942 true] [17592186152074 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375548 13194139535942 true] [17592186152074 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375555 13194139535942 true] [17592186152074 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375590 13194139535942 true] [17592186152075 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375506 13194139535942 true] [17592186152075 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375513 13194139535942 true] [17592186152075 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375520 13194139535942 true] [17592186152075 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375527 13194139535942 true] [17592186152320 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535942 true] [17592186152320 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/c28d3a6098da6c8be022974e96b5242e3bed58ef1347d92571b49c7b521a3b0d.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" 13194139535942 true] [17592186152320 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/q-122847.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x, y)$ defined on $R$. Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" 13194139535942 false] [17592186152320 1145 "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2\\cdot {M}_{12}+{M}_{22} \\\\  & =2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186152634 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535942 true] [17592186152634 1100 "Let $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [- 1, 1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5 - 3x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {-1, 0, 1}, P_{2} = {-1, 0, 1}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$." 13194139535942 false] [17592186152634 1100 "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5-3x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={-1,0,1},\\,{P}_{2}={-1,0,1},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186152634 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/859d9d6b15dbd08b4c015816e46986268f92a94770df866a562f3e379bae5365.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/4c688c7cf529800b8f1ad075dc70784e40a59163330031b375eaad4b1cdd64c9.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6269ba3c4fd853fff89327ab9e6968e2f52439f5c86c0ec5a21dce1620d9ba5d.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-1,-1)=9 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(0,-1)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-1,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(0,0)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =9+6+8+5 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375506 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375507 13194139535942 true] [17592186375506 1089 "q-123049" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375506 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535942 true] [17592186375506 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/76654da8bebc4a156a55bcb6cc7411f4977dfa14634fbceb6e1a7f8e32b2d5b0.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375506 1113 #uuid "aa022868-d2a1-543e-8a08-e76c74c86a57" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375506 1145 "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=6, \\\\ \\text{Area}({R}_{21})=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =6\\cdot {M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375508 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375509 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375510 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375511 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375512 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1041 "selection" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375508 13194139535942 true] [17592186375507 1110 #uuid "ccb9aac3-023f-5d25-bdd6-f731e85c2043" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375508 1034 "6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535942 true] [17592186375508 1111 #uuid "64c68731-d6de-508d-9b6c-62d9edbc2fff" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375509 1034 "48{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}\\cdot {M}_{31}" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535942 true] [17592186375509 1111 #uuid "9dc38173-7018-5176-aae9-3fec91590943" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375510 1034 "{M}_{11}+3{M}_{21}+2{M}_{31}" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535942 true] [17592186375510 1111 #uuid "59cd0e0e-bd7f-5623-9ddb-870eac2d731e" 1319413953