bregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/dfed66710976869aa4507aea2e171c09ac248d4b8b4ca083972dbd10952af6b7.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/a6366799e116eb35e8d3cb5401f0abc161ad183cd8f634bdaf2ce387f64d6c5c.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=25 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=22 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=20 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =25+22+20+17 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375592 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375593 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375594 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375595 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375596 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1041 "selection" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375595 13194139535942 true] [17592186375591 1110 #uuid "9f8f30aa-422b-5742-a14e-63472ce9b8ce" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375592 1034 "74" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535942 true] [17592186375592 1111 #uuid "bc6eccab-a2ca-5f1e-90ec-4c377ec191b2" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375593 1034 "47" 13194139535942 true] [17592186375593 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131